<section class="section" id="why-it-matters" aria-labelledby="why-title">
  <div class="container why-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
      <h2 id="why-title">The file is only useful if the dates are current</h2>
      <p>
        49 CFR Part 391 requires a motor carrier to keep a driver qualification file for each driver.
        The file is not a one-time hire document: medical certificates, MVR pulls, CDL credentials,
        and annual reviews all move forward on their own schedule. When one of those dates slips, the
        gap is usually discovered during a review or a roadside interaction, not on the day it
        happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        A paper folder and a desk calendar can work, but they depend on someone remembering to flip
        the page. A spreadsheet can calculate dates, yet the formula is easy to break and the file is
        often stored somewhere other than the browser you use every day. This tracker keeps the
        arithmetic in one place, shows each document's citation, and puts the next date in front of
        you as soon as you enter the record.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is not to replace your safety consultant or your compliance software. It is to make
        the next date visible for a one-person operation or a small fleet, without creating an account
        or uploading driver information. You can use a nickname, enter only the dates you know, and
        export a backup whenever you want.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="boundaries" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
      <h3 id="boundaries-title">What this tracker does not do</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>It does not determine whether your DQF is complete or compliant.</li>
        <li>It does not replace legal advice, an audit, or a safety consultant.</li>
        <li>It does not track every DQF document, only the six date-based items listed on this page.</li>
        <li>It does not cover random testing, which is a program-level requirement.</li>
        <li>It does not use a server clock; dates are compared with your device date.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        Estimated based on FMCSA Part 391. Not legal advice. Rules can change.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

